Makes TileRange::fromLatLngBounds() name the right tile columns for bounds
whose longitudes lie outside [-180, 180]. It wrapped the west edge into the
world but wrapped the east edge only when it passed 180, so the bounds lost
their width. {{-90, 0}, {90, 360}}, the whole world, covered one column of
four at zoom 2, and {{10, -360}, {20, -350}} cast a negative column to
uint32_t. Region invalidation in custom geometry and custom vector sources, a
source's bounds, and LatLngBounds::contains(CanonicalTileID) all go through
it. The helper now wraps the west edge and keeps the width, and bounds that
reach every column cover the world
(https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-native-ffi/issues/1491).

Finite edges can still be far apart or far out. A width that overflows to
infinity covers the world, and the helper decides that before it builds an
east edge, which LatLng would reject as infinite. The west edge goes through
fmod() before anything that rounds, so a huge longitude keeps its meridian.
An inverted interval, as in LatLngBounds::empty(), counts as its west edge
instead of casting a negative column.
Upstream: not proposed

diff --git a/src/mln/util/tile_range.hpp b/src/mln/util/tile_range.hpp
index c4d68f4..1f9e193 100644
--- a/src/mln/util/tile_range.hpp
+++ b/src/mln/util/tile_range.hpp
@@ -5,6 +5,9 @@
 #include <mln/util/geo.hpp>
 #include <mln/util/projection.hpp>
 
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <cmath>
+
 namespace mln {
 namespace util {
 
@@ -19,13 +22,30 @@ public:
             std::swap(minZoom, maxZoom);
         }
 
-        auto swProj = Projection::project(bounds.southwest().wrapped(), maxZoom);
-        auto ne = bounds.northeast();
-        auto neProj = Projection::project(ne.longitude() > util::LONGITUDE_MAX ? ne.wrapped() : ne, maxZoom);
+        // Wrap the west edge into the world and keep the bounds' width, so
+        // unwrapped longitudes name the same columns as wrapped ones. fmod()
+        // is exact, so it goes first, before any rounding: a huge longitude
+        // still lands on its own meridian. The width of bounds with huge
+        // longitudes can overflow to infinity, which covers the world. An
+        // inverted interval counts as its west edge.
+        const double west = util::wrap(
+            std::fmod(bounds.west(), util::DEGREES_MAX), -util::LONGITUDE_MAX, util::LONGITUDE_MAX);
+        const double width = std::max(bounds.east() - bounds.west(), 0.0);
+        const bool coversWorld = !(width < util::DEGREES_MAX);
+        auto swProj = Projection::project(LatLng(bounds.south(), west), maxZoom);
+        auto neProj = Projection::project(LatLng(bounds.north(), coversWorld ? west : west + width), maxZoom);
 
         const auto maxTile = std::pow(2.0, maxZoom);
-        const auto minX = static_cast<uint32_t>(std::floor(swProj.x));
-        const auto maxX = static_cast<uint32_t>(std::floor(neProj.x));
+        const double westColumn = std::floor(swProj.x);
+        const double eastColumn = std::floor(neProj.x);
+        uint32_t minX = 0;
+        uint32_t maxX = static_cast<uint32_t>(maxTile - 1);
+        // Bounds that reach every column cover the world. Otherwise, bounds
+        // that cross the antimeridian end east of the last column, and wrap.
+        if (!coversWorld && eastColumn - westColumn + 1 < maxTile) {
+            minX = static_cast<uint32_t>(westColumn);
+            maxX = static_cast<uint32_t>(eastColumn < maxTile ? eastColumn : eastColumn - maxTile);
+        }
         const auto minY = static_cast<uint32_t>(util::clamp(std::floor(neProj.y), 0.0, maxTile));
         const auto maxY = static_cast<uint32_t>(util::clamp(std::floor(swProj.y), 0.0, maxTile));
 
